  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1122 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nAn immediate short term weather concern with potential travel\nimpacts is dense fog. There is moist upslope flow from Oakley\nSSE to Medicine Lodge. Feel that as the boundary layer cools\ndown to near or at the dewpoint temperature, dense fog should\nform in the morning. Have already issued a Dense Fog Advisory\nearlier in the shift to account for this hazardous travel\nconcern. It is not totally clear how far west the edge of the\ndense fog will spread. It may reach Dodge City, or it could\nremain to the east of the city. For the rest of the FA, there\nshould be light downslope flow and drier air across western\nKansas. Fog shouldn't be an issue for that second area.\nOtherwise, the fog will likely slow the warming of the boundary\nlayer across the eastern zones. Here, 70's for highs are likely.\nFor the rest of the fog-free area, the dry atmosphere should\nhave no problem warming well into the 80s.\n\nOn Monday, a large upper level low will start to carve out \nacross California. Downstream, across our area, shortwave \nridging will prevail across the Great Plains. The net weather \nresult for Monday is a dry forecast and pleasant temperatures. \nHighs Monday will range from the mid to upper 70s across the \nnorthwest zones to the lower 80s across the southeast zones. \nWinds will eventually become light southeasterly as sfc \npressures begin to fall across the High Plains of Colorado. \n\nAttention then turns to the Tuesday through Thursday period.\nBoth deterministic models as well as their ensemble counterparts\nshow a large upper level low deepening across the southwestern\nUnited States. In addition, it looks like the remnants of\nHurricane Polo will be absorbed in the synoptic low. Looking\ndownstream of these two features, it looks like significant\nmonsoonal and tropical moisture will advect in across Kansas.\nPWATs on some of the models are incredible with values in the\n1.5\" to may be even as high as 2.0\". If you consider that the\naverage PWAT for KDDC for that time of year is 0.74\", you can\nsee how statistically anomalous this incoming PWAT is. The\nbottom line is that the atmosphere will be incredibly primed \nfor a heavy rainfall event across the state. The latest WPC QPF\ngrids are forecasting 2\" to 4\" across southwest Kansas during\nthis period. WPC also has a moderate risk Tuesday and a slight\nrisk Wednesday in their Excessive Rainfall Outlook. Looking at\nother model sets, the LREF has greater than 60% probability of\nrainfall more than one inch. For 2\", the LREF is down to 30-40%\nchance. Feel this might be a little too conservative though.\nExamining numerous ensemble members from the GEFS and ENS, it\nlooks flat out wet across western Kansas. Several of the members\nhave significant rainfall amounts across the entire region.\nBottom line: it is looking very likely that a significant\nrainfall event will occur next week.",
